  <worksheet xml:id="ws-posets-subset-lattice" component="posets-subset">
    <title>Width of the Subset Lattice</title>
    <!-- <page> -->
      <exercise workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Below are diagrams for <m>\mbf{2}^{4}</m> and <m>\mbf{2}^{5}</m>.
            (Without any of the points labeled with corresponding sets.) In the space provided, draw <m>\mbf{2}^{1}</m>, <m>\mbf{2}^{2}</m>, and <m>\mbf{2}^{3}</m>.
            (Label the sets, too.
            You can just write <q><m>12</m></q> instead of <q><m>\{1,2\}</m></q>.) Label a few of the points in the diagrams provided for <m>\mbf{2}^{4}</m> and <m>\mbf{2}^{5}</m> to make sure you understand how to do it.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <image width="40%">
        <description>
          <p>The unlabeled lattice of subsets of <m>\set{1,2,3,4}</m>. The points are displayed in five layers. The bottom layer has a single point. The second layer from the bottom contains four points. The third layer from the bottom contains six points. The fourth layer from the bottom contains four points. The top layer contains a single point.</p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="subset-lattice-4">
          <diagram dimensions="(300,240)" margins="15">
            <definition>graph={0:[1,2,3,4],1:[12,13,14],2:[12,23,24],3:[13,23,34],
              4:[14,24,34],12:[123,124],23:[123,234],24:[124,234],34:[134,234],13:[123,134],
              14:[124,134],
              1234:[123,124,134,234]}</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(1,1,6,5)">
                <network directed="no" graph="graph" arrows="end" scale="0.8" node-fill="#ccc" node-stroke="black" labels="no">
                <node at="0" p="(2,1)"/>
                <node at="1" p="(1,2)"/>
                <node at="2" p="(2,2)"/>
                <node at="3" p="(3,2)"/>
                <node at="4" p="(5,2)"/>
                <node at="12" p="(1,3)"/>
                <node at="13" p="(2,3)"/>
                <node at="14" p="(4,3)"/>
                <node at="23" p="(3,3)"/>
                <node at="24" p="(5,3)"/>
                <node at="34" p="(6,3)"/>
                <node at="123" p="(2,4)"/>
                <node at="124" p="(4,4)"/>
                <node at="134" p="(5,4)"/>
                <node at="234" p="(6,4)"/>
                <node at="1234" p="(5,5)"/>
                
              </network>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <image width="80%">
        <description>
          <p>The unlabeled lattice of subsets of <m>\set{1,2,3,4,5}</m>. The points are displayed in five layers. The bottom layer has a single point. The second layer from the bottom contains five points. The third and fourth layers from the bottom each contain 10 points. The fifth layer from the bottom contains five points. The top layer contains a single point.</p>  
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="subset-lattice-5">
          <diagram dimensions="(600,280)" margins="15">
            <definition>graph={0:[1,2,3,4,5],1:[12,13,14,15],2:[12,23,24,25],3:[13,23,34,35],
              4:[14,24,34,45],12:[123,124,125],23:[123,234,235],24:[124,234,245],34:[134,234,345],
              13:[123,134,135],
              14:[124,134,145],
              1234:[123,124,134,234,12345],
              123:[1235],124:[1245],234:[2345],134:[1345],
              5:[15,25,35,45],15:[125,135,145],25:[125,235,245],35:[135,235,345],
              45:[145,245,345],125:[1235,1245],235:[1235,2345],245:[1245,2345],345:[1345,2345],
              135:[1235,1345],
              145:[1245,1345],
              12345:[1235,1245,1345,2345]}</definition>
